**Theorem 11.1 (static structural consequences at the same root).**
Under Assumption 10.1, the following strict inequalities hold:

$$
\begin{gathered}
0<D_u<D_v,\quad E_{\ell,12}<0,\quad l_u,l_v>0,\quad
l_u>2|E_{\ell,12}|,\\
E_{\ell,11}>0,\quad E_{\ell,22}-E_{\ell,11}>0,\quad
4E_{\ell,11}(E_{\ell,22}-E_{\ell,11})>l_v^2,\\
\ell(h)>0\quad\hbox{whenever }u\ge0.
\end{gathered}
\tag{FC.29}
$$

Proof. All decimals in this proof are exact terminating rationals.
GC Definition 6.1, equivalently the source rows of
[ST Definition 10.1](FIB_NATIVE_COMPLETE_CONTINUATION_SATURATED_CONTACT_TRANSITIONS.md),
implies the following wider open rational intervals, by comparing
each dyadic endpoint with the displayed rational endpoint:

| Source coordinate | Open rational interval |
| --- | --- |
| $h_0$ | $(0.984,0.985)$ |
| $h_1$ | $(0.706,0.707)$ |
| $h_2$ | $(0.809,0.810)$ |
| $a$ | $(0.0656,0.0657)$ |
| $b$ | $(0.1459,0.1461)$ |
| $c_{\rm src}$ | $(0.0154,0.0156)$ |
| $d$ | $(0.0987,0.0989)$ |

Use the exact matrix (GC.28). With $q_*=11/64$, direct trace
multiplication gives all entries of the symmetric $N$:

$$
\begin{gathered}
N_{00}=q_*(h_0^2-h_1^2),\quad
N_{11}=q_*(b^2-a^2),\quad N_{22}=q_*d^2,\quad
N_{12}=ac_{\rm src}\Delta_{12},\\
N_{01}=(h_0a+h_1b)\Delta_{01},\qquad
N_{02}=(h_0c_{\rm src}+h_2d)\Delta_{02},\qquad N_{ji}=N_{ij},\\
\Delta_{01}=(\sqrt{999}-\sqrt{295})/128,\quad
\Delta_{02}=(\sqrt{27}-\sqrt5)/16,\quad
\Delta_{12}=(\sqrt{37}-\sqrt{59})/16.
\end{gathered}
\tag{FC.30}
$$

For explicit rational comparisons one may use
$31.606<\sqrt{999}<31.607$, $17.175<\sqrt{295}<17.176$,
$5.196<\sqrt{27}<5.197$, $2.236<\sqrt5<2.237$,
$6.082<\sqrt{37}<6.083$, and $7.681<\sqrt{59}<7.682$.
Each assertion follows by squaring its positive endpoints. In
particular $\Delta_{01}>0.112$,
$0<\Delta_{02}<0.186$ and $-0.101<\Delta_{12}<0$.

The cancelled ratio

$$
\alpha=\frac1{1+c_{\rm src}dh_1/(abh_2)},\qquad
\beta=1-\alpha,\qquad
\delta=\frac{ab}{h_0h_1}+\frac{c_{\rm src}d}{h_0h_2}
\tag{FC.31}
$$

avoids treating a repeated numerator and denominator as unrelated.
For instance the lower and upper bounds for $\alpha$ are
$[1+0.0156\cdot0.0989\cdot0.707/
(0.0656\cdot0.1459\cdot0.809)]^{-1}$ and
$[1+0.0154\cdot0.0987\cdot0.706/
(0.0657\cdot0.1461\cdot0.810)]^{-1}$.
Positive rational multiplication and division in (FC.31) give

$$
0.876<\alpha<0.879,\qquad 0.121<\beta<0.124,\qquad
0.0156<\delta<0.0159.
\tag{FC.32}
$$

Here are sufficient endpoint comparisons for all the structural
height bounds, using $G=LNL$. Put

$$
\begin{aligned}
L_{11}&=q_*\left[0.0156(0.984^2-0.707^2)
                 +(0.1459^2-0.0657^2)/0.879\right],\\
U_{11}&=q_*\left[0.0159(0.985^2-0.706^2)
                 +(0.1461^2-0.0656^2)/0.876\right],\\
L_{\rm gap}&=q_*\left[0.0987^2/0.124
                 -(0.1461^2-0.0656^2)/0.876\right].
\end{aligned}
\tag{FC.33}
$$

Cross multiplication of positive denominators gives
$0.00456<L_{11}<E_{\ell,11}<U_{11}<0.00465$ and
$E_{\ell,22}-E_{\ell,11}>L_{\rm gap}>0.01015$.
The latter difference cancels $G_{00}$ exactly.
Also $\sqrt{\alpha\beta}>0.325$,
$\sqrt{\delta/\alpha}>0.133$ and
$\sqrt{\delta/\beta}<0.363$, by (FC.32) and squared rational
comparisons. Consequently

$$
\begin{aligned}
0<-E_{\ell,12}
&<0.0657\cdot0.0156\cdot0.101/0.325
 =0.0003185136<0.00032,\\
l_u&>2(0.133)(0.984\cdot0.0656+0.706\cdot0.1459)(0.112)
 =0.0049918223936>0.0049,\\
0<l_v&<2(0.363)(0.985\cdot0.0156+0.810\cdot0.0989)(0.186)
 =0.0128925621<0.013.
\end{aligned}
\tag{FC.34}
$$

Further, $b^2/\alpha<0.1461^2/0.876<0.025$, whereas
$d^2/\beta>0.0987^2/0.124>0.078$. This proves $D_u<D_v$.
The signs of $E_{\ell,12},l_u,l_v$ also follow directly from
(FC.30). Finally

$$
4(0.00456)(0.01015)=0.000185136>0.000169=0.013^2,
\qquad 0.0049>2(0.00032).
\tag{FC.35}
$$

For $u\ge0$, the term $u(l_u+2E_{\ell,12}v)$ is nonnegative.
Writing $\gamma_\ell=E_{\ell,22}-E_{\ell,11}>0$ therefore gives

$$
\ell\ge E_{\ell,11}+\gamma_\ell v^2+l_vv
\ge E_{\ell,11}-\frac{l_v^2}{4\gamma_\ell}>0.
\tag{FC.36}
$$

This includes $u=0$. These are consequences of enclosure membership
for the same source expressions. They neither isolate a root nor
prove TP at the corners or other points of the enclosing box.
$\square$

